Let *A, B, C,* and *D* be as many numbers as we please in proportion, so that *A* is to *B* as *C* is to *D.*

I say that *A* is to *B* as the sum of *A* and *C* is to the sum of *B* and *D.*

Since *A* is to *B* as *C* is to *D,* therefore *A* is the same part or parts of *B* as *C* is of *D.* Therefore the sum of *A* and *C* is the same part or parts of the sum of *B* and *D* that *A* is of *B.* [!just VII.Def.20; VII.5, VII.6]

Therefore *A* is to *B* as the sum of *A* and *C* is to the sum of *B* and *D.* [!just VII.Def.20]

Therefore, *if any number of numbers are proportional, then one of the antecedents is to one of the consequents as the sum of the antecedents is to the sum of the consequents.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
---
body:

This proposition is the numerical analogue of @V.12. Symbolically,

<center>if <i>a</i><sub>1</sub> : <i>b</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> : <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> = ... = <i>a<sub>n</sub></i> : <i>b<sub>n</sub>,</i></center>

then each of these ratios also equals the ratio

<center>(<i>a</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>a</i><sub>2</sub> + ... + <i>a<sub>n</sub></i>) : (<i>b</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>b</i><sub>2</sub> + ... + <i>b<sub>n</sub></i>).</center>

Euclid takes *n* to be 2 in his proof.

This proposition is used in @VII.15 , @VII.20, and @IX.35.
